      "math_academy_id": "e-8453",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(2x-2)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(2x-2)^2}\\textrm{d}x = \n\\int \\dfrac{1}{4-4(x-1)^2}\\textrm{d}x = \n\\dfrac{1}{4} {\\int}\\dfrac{1}{ 1 - (x-1)^2 }\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(x-1)}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|{x-1} \\right| >   1.$",
